WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:05.700
From tearful runny noses to iconic&nbsp;
improvised lines anyone can quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:09.014
these monologues are pure movie magic!

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:14.760
Charlie Chaplin's speech in "The Great Dictator"&nbsp;
is often listed among the great movie monologues,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:19.440
and for good reason. It's hard to think of a&nbsp;
better speech than this one, as it perfectly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:24.120
encapsulates the hopeful spirit of the movie while&nbsp;
also making an important political statement.

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:29.640
"The Great Dictator" is Chaplin's first sound&nbsp;
film and is one of the greatest satires of all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:34.620
time. Chaplin plays two characters –- a Jewish&nbsp;
barber and former soldier and a Nazi dictator&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:39.840
named Adenoid Hynkel, a parody of Adolf&nbsp;
Hitler. The Barber and Hynkel look alike,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:43.380
of course, and at one point in the&nbsp;
film Hynkel's men mistake him for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:46.740
the Barber and arrest him, causing&nbsp;
the Barber to take Hynkel's place.

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:49.200
The famous monologue comes at the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:53.340
as the Barber, dressed as Hynkel, makes&nbsp;
an impassioned speech to the public.

00:00:53.518 --> 00:00:58.018
"Do not despair. The misery that is now&nbsp;
upon us is but the passing of greed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.018 --> 00:01:01.501
the bitterness of men who fear&nbsp;
the way of human progress."

00:01:01.501 --> 00:01:05.520
He urges the soldiers to rise&nbsp;
up and defy their masters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:09.540
the people of the world to embrace&nbsp;
kindness instead of hate, and those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:15.000
currently in bondage to have hope. The fact&nbsp;
that the film was released in 1940 –- after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:20.400
the Nazis had already risen to power -– makes&nbsp;
this scene even more impactful, but Chaplin's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:27.240
message is just as important today as it was back&nbsp;
then. It's a perfect speech and true movie magic.

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:31.477
"If I'd have put him down I'd have had&nbsp;
a title shot. I was ready that night."

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:37.320
Marlon Brando is a master of the monologue,&nbsp;
so it's no surprise that two of his films&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:42.240
feature on this list. One of his most famous&nbsp;
pieces of dialogue comes from a film in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:47.520
early days of his career, 1954's "On the&nbsp;
Waterfront." Brando plays Terry Malloy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:51.840
a former champion boxer who now works as&nbsp;
a longshoreman in New Jersey. His career&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:55.800
went up in smoke when mob boss Johnny&nbsp;
Friendly convinced him to throw a fight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.800 --> 00:02:00.240
and now he's mustering up the courage&nbsp;
to testify against Friendly in court.

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:04.560
In the most famous scene in the film, Terry is&nbsp;
in the back of a car with his older brother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:08.220
Charley. Terry reminds Charley that&nbsp;
he was the one who told Terry to throw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:12.182
the match on behalf of Friendly,&nbsp;
something he still blames him for.

00:02:12.490 --> 00:02:16.318
"I coulda been a contender,&nbsp;
I coulda been somebody."

00:02:16.318 --> 00:02:20.520
Despite Brando's slurred drawl,&nbsp;
the lines are crystal clear,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:26.460
and the despair in his voice is right there&nbsp;
on the surface. The scene is quite brief,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:31.800
but Brando reveals everything we need to know&nbsp;
about the character here -– his bitterness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:37.320
his longing for a different life, and how he's&nbsp;
been living all this time with a broken heart.

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:42.720
Harper Lee's "To Kill a Mockingbird" is often&nbsp;
considered one of the great American novels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:49.260
and the film adaptation is held in similarly high&nbsp;
regard. Gregory Peck plays Atticus Finch, a single&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:54.960
father, and principled lawyer. Finch takes on&nbsp;
the case of Tom Robinson, a Black man accused&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.960 --> 00:03:01.140
of raping a white woman. Finch knows Robinson has&nbsp;
very little chance of winning the case due to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:05.880
deep-seated racism of the South, but he puts up&nbsp;
an impassioned effort to get him off anyways.

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:10.740
In his final speech to the courtroom,&nbsp;
Finch knows he's fighting a losing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:15.360
case and he's all but lost the jury.&nbsp;
He's successfully proven Robinson's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:19.534
innocence but is struggling to change the&nbsp;
hearts and minds of his fellow citizens.

00:03:19.534 --> 00:03:27.094
"In our courts, all men are created equal."

00:03:27.094 --> 00:03:31.080
He urges the jury to do their&nbsp;
duty and follow the evidence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:35.280
not their prejudice, though he&nbsp;
knows they very likely will not.

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:40.140
It's a powerhouse performance by Peck,&nbsp;
and it's considered some of his best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:44.760
work. The Guardian reports that novelist&nbsp;
Harper Lee was so happy with his take on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:49.020
Finch –- who was based on her own father&nbsp;
–- that she gave him her father's old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:53.340
pocket watch. This powerful courtroom&nbsp;
scene proves what an impressive actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:58.140
Peck is and why the film deserves its&nbsp;
elevated status in the cinematic canon.

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:00.544
"You're gonna need a bigger boat."

00:04:00.544 --> 00:04:06.300
The making of Steven Spielberg's "Jaws" is&nbsp;
legendary. Bruce the shark kept malfunctioning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:12.660
there was major tension on set, and the film went&nbsp;
way over budget. Still, despite these troubles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:18.540
the film contains one of the greatest movie&nbsp;
monologues of all time, a scene that is also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:22.860
a favorite of Spielberg's, according to Den&nbsp;
of Geek. The monologue is delivered by Quint,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.860 --> 00:04:27.660
the grizzled, Captain Ahab-like shark hunter who&nbsp;
joins Hooper and Brody on their boat, the Orca.

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:31.860
Sitting in the cabin one evening, Quint&nbsp;
shares the harrowing real-life story of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:38.398
the USS Indianapolis, an event that mirrors their&nbsp;
own predicament. He describes the 1945 disaster,

00:04:38.398 --> 00:04:44.458
"1,100 men went in the water, 316 men&nbsp;
come out, and the sharks took the rest."

00:04:44.458 --> 00:04:48.180
Spielberg explained in a documentary&nbsp;
that the scene was a "Rosetta Stone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:50.820
for Quint's entire character" because it reveals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:54.600
all of his motivations and why he&nbsp;
feels so strongly about sharks.

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:59.340
The scene is delivered with a perfectly&nbsp;
garbled drawl by Robert Shaw, who,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:03.960
according to co-screenwriter Carl Gottlieb,&nbsp;
actually re-wrote much of the scene himself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:09.060
As evidenced by the shocked faces of Hooper&nbsp;
and Brody, Quint's tale has a distressing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:14.460
effect on his companions, as they can only&nbsp;
imagine the horrors he's experienced. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:20.160
the most grounded, restrained scene in a&nbsp;
movie that is often said to have invented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:25.000
the crowd-pleasing summer blockbuster,&nbsp;
and it's among Spielberg's best work.

00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:28.380
"Network" is one of the great films of the 1970s,

00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:33.900
and its brilliant take on the dissolution of&nbsp;
television ethics is still relevant today. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:38.160
film follows old-school news anchor Howard&nbsp;
Beale, who's forced into retirement after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:43.440
a drop in the ratings. Beale is incensed&nbsp;
and goes on a televised rant in front of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:48.810
the nation. The most popular monologue in&nbsp;
the film is probably Beale's famous tirade:

00:05:48.810 --> 00:05:52.950
"I'm as mad as hell, and I'm&nbsp;
not gonna take this anymore!"

00:05:52.950 --> 00:05:57.240
Still, the most compelling monologue in&nbsp;
the film comes closer to the end when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.240 --> 00:06:01.080
Beale meets with Arthur Jensen, the chairman&nbsp;
of the conglomerate that owns the network.

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:06.240
Jensen tries to convince Beale to abandon&nbsp;
his anti-capitalist sermon by teaching him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:13.140
how the world really works. Jensen, at the&nbsp;
far end of a very long table, tells Beale,

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:20.460
"You have meddled with the primal forces&nbsp;
of nature Mr. Beale and I won't have it!"

00:06:20.460 --> 00:06:27.780
He adds that there is only one natural force that&nbsp;
rules the world: money. It's an extremely bleak&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:32.580
sentiment, but his booming voice and the unique&nbsp;
way the scene is shot –- with the camera getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.580 --> 00:06:38.220
closer and closer to Jensen as the monologue goes&nbsp;
on — makes it impossible to tear your eyes away.

00:06:38.760 --> 00:06:42.720
Francis Ford Coppola's "Apocalypse Now"&nbsp;
is one of the most famous movies ever,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:47.160
perhaps partly because the behind-the-scenes&nbsp;
drama has become so legendary. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:52.080
despite the harrowing circumstances of the film's&nbsp;
production, Coppola was able to capture some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:57.000
the greatest scenes in film history. There are a&nbsp;
number of celebrated moments in "Apocalypse Now,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:02.220
but one monologue, in particular, stands&nbsp;
out. Marlon Brando was famously difficult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:07.620
to work with on set, but all of that strife&nbsp;
resulted in a scene involving Colonel Kurtz.

00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:12.000
Bathed in shadow, the rogue Colonel&nbsp;
Kurtz recounts the horrors of war,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:15.720
reflecting on the circumstances that&nbsp;
have brought him to the jungle where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:20.760
he now rules his own small kingdom. Kurtz&nbsp;
tells Martin Sheen's, Captain Willard,

00:07:20.868 --> 00:07:27.120
"I've seen horrors. Horrors that&nbsp;
you've seen. But you have no right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:29.400
to call me a murderer, you&nbsp;
have a right to kill me."

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:34.860
He goes on to describe these horrors in detail,&nbsp;
explaining how he felt when he saw children who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.860 --> 00:07:39.000
had their arms chopped off and thrown in&nbsp;
a pile. Having seen the worst of humanity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:45.480
Kurtz has abandoned his own sense of morality and&nbsp;
descended into a kind of principled insanity. Much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.480 --> 00:07:50.400
of Kurtz's dialogue was based on Brando's&nbsp;
own improvisations, and, despite the effort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:55.445
that clearly went into the complicated&nbsp;
performance, the scene feels effortless.

00:07:55.445 --> 00:08:00.420
Film characters don't often have the chance to&nbsp;
make philosophical speeches just before their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.420 --> 00:08:05.820
deaths, but in a world filled with robots, things&nbsp;
work a little differently. The original "Blade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.820 --> 00:08:11.430
Runner" film gives us one of the greatest sci-fi&nbsp;
monologues of all time, delivered by a replicant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.430 --> 00:08:17.520
-– a humanoid robot -– named Roy Batty. The film&nbsp;
follows Rick Deckard, a former Blade Runner whose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:21.600
job was to hunt down and kill replicants,&nbsp;
who are now seen as a threat to humanity.

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:24.900
Batty is one of the rogue replicants&nbsp;
that Deckard has been sent to kill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.900 --> 00:08:30.180
and his final scene is one of the greatest&nbsp;
sci-moments ever captured on film. Batty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.180 --> 00:08:35.401
saves Deckard's life and then, realizing his time&nbsp;
is up, reflects on the nature of his existence.

00:08:35.401 --> 00:08:41.719
"I've seen things you people wouldn't believe."

00:08:41.719 --> 00:08:45.360
He describes the amazing things&nbsp;
he's seen in his short lifetime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:47.580
summing up the experience by saying,

00:08:47.580 --> 00:08:51.540
"All those moments will be lost&nbsp;
in time. Like tears in rain."

00:08:51.540 --> 00:08:57.480
The monologue itself is only 42 words&nbsp;
long, but it packs an incredible punch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.480 --> 00:09:02.580
aided by Rutger Hauer's magnificent&nbsp;
performance and a quietly soaring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.580 --> 00:09:07.020
soundtrack. Hauer told Radio Times that he&nbsp;
actually re-wrote parts of the scene himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.020 --> 00:09:11.820
including that iconic final line. It's arguably&nbsp;
the most important moment in the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.820 --> 00:09:18.060
as it proves just how human the replicants&nbsp;
really are. One death scene to rule them all.

00:09:18.350 --> 00:09:25.320
Wim Wenders' 1984 film "Paris, Texas" is about a&nbsp;
man lost at sea. Well, more precisely it's about a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.320 --> 00:09:29.700
man lost in the desert, but the metaphor is still&nbsp;
apt. Harry Dean Stanton plays Travis Henderson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.700 --> 00:09:34.440
a man who has been missing for many years.&nbsp;
The recently rescued Travis reconnects with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:39.180
his brother and son but soon finds himself on a&nbsp;
journey to locate the mother of his child, Jane.

00:09:39.180 --> 00:09:43.800
He finds Jane in Houston at the peep-show&nbsp;
club where she works. He sees her behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.800 --> 00:09:48.780
a one-way mirror and decides to finally&nbsp;
speak to her. Travis tells Jane their story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.780 --> 00:09:53.460
starting with the time they met and fell in&nbsp;
love and ending with the violent dissolution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.460 --> 00:09:57.900
of their union. The 10-minute-long scene&nbsp;
is beautifully directed by Wenders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.900 --> 00:10:01.860
who alternates between focusing the&nbsp;
camera on Travis as he's telling the tale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.860 --> 00:10:06.600
and Jane, who slowly begins to&nbsp;
realize the story is about her.

00:10:06.955 --> 00:10:09.235
"Sounds like they were very happy."

00:10:11.259 --> 00:10:13.779
"Yes, they were. They were real happy."

00:10:13.779 --> 00:10:19.680
It's a masterclass in empathetic storytelling as&nbsp;
it's difficult not to feel for both characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:25.500
even as Travis describes the terrible way he&nbsp;
treated Jane. "Paris, Texas" won the Palme&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.500 --> 00:10:30.960
d'Or at the Cannes Film Festival in 1984,&nbsp;
and this scene is likely a huge reason why.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.960 --> 00:10:38.204
A slow-burning monologue filled with resignation&nbsp;
and regret, it's an enormously affecting moment.

00:10:38.220 --> 00:10:43.200
Many of the great monologues in film&nbsp;
history come from equally great films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.200 --> 00:10:48.000
but this isn’t always the case. In his&nbsp;
storied career, Al Pacino has delivered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:52.800
countless powerful speeches in movies like&nbsp;
"The Godfather" and "Scarface." However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.800 --> 00:10:59.160
one of his most celebrated monologues is from a&nbsp;
film that is often forgotten — 1992's "Scent of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.160 --> 00:11:04.920
a Woman." Pacino plays Lieutenant Colonel Frank&nbsp;
Slade, a blind, cantankerous Vietnam veteran&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.920 --> 00:11:09.540
planning to end his life. Frank's niece&nbsp;
hires Charlie, a prep student in trouble&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.540 --> 00:11:13.320
for refusing to rat out his classmates,&nbsp;
to look after Frank over Thanksgiving.

00:11:13.320 --> 00:11:18.720
Frank spends much of the movie acting like&nbsp;
a mean, angry old man, but he swoops in at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.720 --> 00:11:23.220
the end to save the day. Charlie is forced to sit&nbsp;
through a hearing led by his crooked headmaster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.220 --> 00:11:28.560
who thinks he should be expelled for refusing&nbsp;
to inform on his peers. Frank then enters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:33.480
the courtroom and gives an impassioned speech&nbsp;
about the importance of courage and integrity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.480 --> 00:11:36.480
qualities which seem to have&nbsp;
been lost on the headmaster.

00:11:37.020 --> 00:11:41.580
Pacino is known for turning things up to 11&nbsp;
in climactic moments — and his performance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.580 --> 00:11:47.880
here isn't exactly subdued — but he gives Frank's&nbsp;
righteous speech a rousing dramatic flair. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.880 --> 00:11:53.520
a classic Hollywood monologue that stands the&nbsp;
test of time, even if the film itself has not.

00:11:53.520 --> 00:11:58.898
"I'll show you out of order! You don't&nbsp;
know what 'out of order' is Mr. Trask."

00:11:58.898 --> 00:12:04.140
Robin Williams is something of a master&nbsp;
advice-giver in films, and nowhere is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.140 --> 00:12:09.000
this more apparent than in the 1997 film "Good&nbsp;
Will Hunting." Williams plays ​​Sean Maguire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:12.780
a therapist who is counseling a young&nbsp;
genius mathematician named Will Hunting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.780 --> 00:12:17.280
played by Matt Damon. Williams has&nbsp;
several impactful monologues in just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.280 --> 00:12:19.980
this film alone, but there's one that stands out.

00:12:19.980 --> 00:12:24.120
Though Sean has been working to break through&nbsp;
Will's defense mechanisms and get to know the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.120 --> 00:12:29.940
real him, Will continues to treat therapy like&nbsp;
a joke and even goes so far as to insult Sean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.940 --> 00:12:35.880
and his dead wife. Sean and Will sit together&nbsp;
on a park bench one day and Sean takes Will to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.880 --> 00:12:40.500
task for his childish arrogance. He tells&nbsp;
Will that he may know things about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.500 --> 00:12:45.360
world from reading books, but he's never&nbsp;
actually lived in the world, as Sean has.

00:12:45.534 --> 00:12:49.314
"I can't learn anything from you,&nbsp;
I can't read in some f---ing book.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.563 --> 00:12:53.123
Unless you want to talk about you."

00:12:53.123 --> 00:12:58.860
It's a talking-down-to that Will rightly&nbsp;
deserves, and it eventually leads to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.860 --> 00:13:03.639
famous therapy breakthrough scene later in the&nbsp;
film when Will admits to being abused as a child.

00:13:03.639 --> 00:13:08.088
"Look at me son. It's not your fault."

00:13:08.088 --> 00:13:13.440
The latter scene is the more memorable one,&nbsp;
but this earlier scene is an important building&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.440 --> 00:13:18.768
block for the film as a whole, and Williams&nbsp;
gives a typically wisened performance here.

00:13:18.780 --> 00:13:23.640
Denzel Washington is one of the great&nbsp;
contemporary masters of the movie monologue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.640 --> 00:13:28.740
and you don't have to look far to find&nbsp;
some legendary ones. From "Malcolm X" to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.740 --> 00:13:34.500
"Philadelphia" to "Macbeth," he's an actor of&nbsp;
almost unparalleled strength and gravitas. One&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.500 --> 00:13:39.480
of his very best monologues is not a political&nbsp;
speech or a drawn-out soliloquy but a short,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.480 --> 00:13:44.700
volatile scene from the 2001 film "Training&nbsp;
Day." Washington plays Alonzo Harris,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.700 --> 00:13:49.620
a crooked LAPD cop who’s forced to start&nbsp;
working with a rookie, played by Ethan Hawke.

00:13:49.620 --> 00:13:53.700
By the time we get to Washington's&nbsp;
greatest monologue, Alonzo's time has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.700 --> 00:13:58.200
started to run out. Though he previously&nbsp;
ruled the neighborhood with an iron fist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.200 --> 00:14:03.960
his hold on the community is starting to slip, and&nbsp;
the Russian mob has a hit out on him. He tries to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.960 --> 00:14:08.280
reassert his dominance by giving a threatening&nbsp;
speech to the local gang members and onlookers.

00:14:08.280 --> 00:14:13.440
"I'ma burn this motherf---er down.&nbsp;
King Kong ain't got s--- on me!"

00:14:13.440 --> 00:14:20.280
"Training Day" may be a gritty crime movie,&nbsp;
but this scene feels very Shakespearian in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.280 --> 00:14:25.500
nature — which makes sense, considering Washington&nbsp;
is a Shakespeare-trained actor. Though he's trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:25.500 --> 00:14:31.500
to remain an alpha, you can hear the desperation&nbsp;
behind his threats, and Washington's dramatic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.500 --> 00:14:36.780
rhythm is spot-on. Washington's work here is&nbsp;
made all the more impressive when you consider&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.780 --> 00:14:43.020
that he improvised the famous "King Kong" line,&nbsp;
which marks the powerful climax in this speech.

00:14:43.680 --> 00:14:48.480
Paul Thomas Anderson is a filmmaking legend&nbsp;
with numerous beloved films under his belt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.480 --> 00:14:53.400
but one scene, and one line of&nbsp;
dialogue, stands above them all.

00:14:53.400 --> 00:14:59.760
Daniel Day Lewis' delivery of this line from&nbsp;
the 2007 film "There Will Be Blood" is one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.760 --> 00:15:06.900
the most iconic lines of the 21st century and&nbsp;
is a small part of a great cinematic monologue.

00:15:06.900 --> 00:15:11.820
The line comes in the film's final scene&nbsp;
as two rivals face off. Eli Sunday is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.820 --> 00:15:15.540
preacher who has long tried to seize power&nbsp;
from the megalomaniac Daniel Plainview.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.540 --> 00:15:19.860
Daniel holds a grudge against Eli because&nbsp;
of the time Eli forced him into a baptism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.860 --> 00:15:24.960
so when Eli comes to Daniel's door like&nbsp;
a beggar, Daniel can't help but point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.960 --> 00:15:29.400
out his hypocrisy. Eli offers to sell&nbsp;
a piece of his oil-rich land to Daniel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.400 --> 00:15:34.751
and Daniel tells him he's already drained&nbsp;
the land, leading to this famous line:

00:15:34.751 --> 00:15:39.011
"I drink your milkshake"

00:15:39.011 --> 00:15:43.680
Eli made a huge mistake coming to&nbsp;
Daniel like this, as Daniel has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:43.680 --> 00:15:49.020
reverted into the worst version&nbsp;
of himself –- misanthropic, cruel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:49.020 --> 00:15:55.560
and bloated with wealth. While the milkshake line&nbsp;
is incredible, the cruelest line comes earlier,

00:15:55.600 --> 00:15:59.080
"You're just an afterbirth, Eli.”

00:15:59.080 --> 00:15:59.580
“No.”

00:15:59.580 --> 00:16:02.280
“You slithered out of your mother's filth."

00:16:02.280 --> 00:16:05.880
The scene ends with Daniel&nbsp;
killing Eli with a bowling pin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.880 --> 00:16:12.141
completing Daniel's transformation into&nbsp;
a man devoid of humanity. Chilling stuff.

00:16:12.540 --> 00:16:19.200
Any movie monologue list needs at least one Viola&nbsp;
Davis scene, and her performance in "Fences" is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.200 --> 00:16:24.780
among her best. If you weren't convinced of Davis'&nbsp;
brilliance before this movie, you certainly will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.780 --> 00:16:29.820
be afterward. "Fences" is an adaptation of the&nbsp;
August Wilson play of the same name and follows a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:29.820 --> 00:16:34.860
married couple, Troy and Rose, along with their&nbsp;
son, Cory. Troy never got over his dream of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.860 --> 00:16:40.020
becoming a professional baseball player, and he&nbsp;
takes his frustrations out on his wife and son.

00:16:40.020 --> 00:16:43.680
In one climactic scene, Rose confronts Troy about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:43.680 --> 00:16:48.300
his cheating and he tries to justify&nbsp;
it by explaining how hard it's been,

00:16:49.020 --> 00:16:53.301
"It's not easy for me to admit that I've been&nbsp;
standing in the same place for 18 years.”

00:16:53.301 --> 00:16:55.080
“Well, I've been standing with you!"

00:16:55.080 --> 00:17:00.180
With tears streaming down her face and&nbsp;
snot literally dripping into her mouth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.180 --> 00:17:06.000
Rose lays out her desolation, explaining how she&nbsp;
had to bury her wants and needs to stay married&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.000 --> 00:17:10.740
to Troy and find a reason to keep living.&nbsp;
Troy doesn't reflect on Rose's feelings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:10.740 --> 00:17:14.760
of course, but she lays them&nbsp;
out with devastating clarity.

00:17:14.760 --> 00:17:18.840
"I took all my feelings - my&nbsp;
wants and needs and dreams."

00:17:18.840 --> 00:17:21.960
“I planted myself inside you and waited to bloom."

00:17:21.960 --> 00:17:27.420
Davis gives a tour de force performance here&nbsp;
–- the kind you have to remind yourself to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:27.420 --> 00:17:32.040
breathe while watching –- proving that&nbsp;
she's really in a league of her own.

